    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe disposable-domain rejection follows the same pipeline pattern the codebase already uses for every other owner validation rule \u2014 one small step class, one checked exception, one handler, one escalation mapping \u2014 with the domain-matching logic living in the shared email helper.\n\n1. **`OwnerEmails.java`** \u2014 added `isDisposableDomain(email)` alongside the existing `normalize`/`isValid`, keeping all email string logic in the one helper. The blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) is a static `Set`, and the domain is lower-cased before lookup.\n\n2. **`RequireOwnerEmailAllowed.java`** (new step) \u2014 a `Require`-style validator that reads the published `OwnerFieldsDto`, skips absent emails, and throws `DisposableEmailException` when the domain is blocklisted. It runs *after* `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, so it checks the canonical lower-cased value.\n\n3. **`DisposableEmailException` + `DisposableEmailExceptionHandler`** (new) \u2014 a checked exception and a handler that delegates to the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` to respond 400, mirroring `InvalidEmailException`.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `...DisposableEmailException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted a `requireEmailAllowed` step right after `normalizeEmail` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml`, so both create and update reject disposable domains.\n\nThe new `errorRejectsDisposableDomain()` test (POST with `x@mailinator.com` \u2192 400) passes, and no existing tests regressed.",
